    "difficulty": "hard",
    "item_data_json": "{\"options\":{\"A\":\"Approach within arm’s reach while another nurse observes and begin assessing the trigger, because close therapeutic presence can reduce escalating agitation safely.\",\"B\":\"Administer a PRN sedative before changing the environment, because medication should precede other de-escalation measures when an object is being held.\",\"C\":\"Leave the client in the day room but move staff farther away and wait for spontaneous calming, because additional interaction may intensify agitation before assessment.\",\"D\":\"Increase distance, clear nearby people from immediate danger, call for support, and use a calm de-escalation approach while maintaining a safe exit route.\"}}",
    "item_type_raw": "multiple_choice",
    "mode": "standalone",
    "official_case_slot": null,
    "original_sequence": null,
    "question_uid": "V2-Q0051",
    "rationale": "When a person is escalating while holding an object that could be used as a weapon, immediate scene safety precedes detailed exploration. The nurse should create distance, move bystanders out of danger, summon assistance, preserve an exit route, and use calm de-escalation. Approaching within striking distance, waiting in a crowded environment, or treating medication as the automatic first intervention can increase risk.",
